deploying.md: serving member sites off a wildcard subdomain
Your publish-to-main plan, with the two things that would otherwise be found at
the dashboard. One section in `docs/deploying.md`. No code.

## The plan lands on the existing pattern cleanly

Built member sites committed at `site/member-sites/<member>/`, wildcard
subdomain rewritten onto that prefix. Committing the built tree is what the five
`_data` syncs already do — write the output, commit it, let the commit be the
record — at a larger size. Your *"big chunky commit every month."*

## Measured: it has to be under `site/`, and nothing would tell you

**The build root is `site/`.** A `member-sites/` at the repository root is not
copied into `_site` and is served by nothing — **and no error appears**, because
a root directory being unpublished is the *correct* behaviour that
`site/bin/test_nothing_internal_is_published.py` exists to guarantee. You would
commit a tree, deploy, and get 404s with a green build.

So it goes inside the build root, where it is content rather than station.

**And Jekyll passes built HTML through untouched.** I put
`{{ site.title }}` and `{% if x %}y{% endif %}` in a file at
`site/member-sites/probe/index.html` and built: **byte-for-byte identical** out
the other side. Liquid only applies to files with YAML front matter, and built
output has none, so a member's page cannot be mangled by our build even when it
contains text that looks like a template.

That measurement is why one build and one deploy is enough.

## Measured: the rewrite needs a Worker, and the free tool cannot do it

**A URL Rewrite under Transform Rules is the obvious answer and it will not
work.** Transform Rules exist on every plan — 10 active on Free — but
**regex is Business and Enterprise only**. Turning
`<member>.fcpublicmedia.org/x` into `/member-sites/<member>/x` means capturing a
label out of the hostname into the path, which is inherently dynamic. No number
of static rules substitutes.

**And the default serving order is backwards.** With assets and a Worker script
both configured, *"Cloudflare will first attempt to serve static assets if one
matches the incoming request."* A request for `<member>.fcpublicmedia.org/`
matches the root `index.html` — **FCPM's own homepage** — so every member
subdomain would serve our front page, and nothing would look broken enough to
investigate.

Two fixes, trading against different things:

| | how | costs |
|---|---|---|
| **One Worker** | `main` + `"run_worker_first": true` in `site/wrangler.jsonc`; the script reads the hostname and rewrites the path | the main site stops being *"no main, no runtime, nothing to execute"* — its own `wrangler.jsonc` comment celebrates exactly that |
| **Two Workers** | `site/` stays pure assets; a second Worker on `*.fcpublicmedia.org/*` with the built sites as its own asset directory | a second deploy, and the sites are not also reachable as a path on the main domain |

**The one-Worker option is the one you described**, and there is a reason to
prefer it beyond that: the listing only sits beside the sites if the sites are
inside `site/`, and you wanted that index — *"the index file in this listing of
submodules, like, right next to them, is exactly what we could publish on our
own site."*

Note `run_worker_first` also takes an array of paths, but the discriminator here
is the **hostname**, not the path, so it has to be `true`.

## And one detail that will bite: `baseurl`

The member build deliberately sets no `baseurl`, which is correct on a subdomain
served by a **rewrite** — the browser stays on `<member>.fcpublicmedia.org`, so
`/feed.xml` resolves there and gets rewritten too.

The consequence: **the same build is wrong at
`www.fcpublicmedia.org/member-sites/<member>/`**, where every link and
stylesheet would 404 against the main site's root. If both addresses should
work, the path form needs its own build with `baseurl` set, or it redirects to
the subdomain. Worth deciding before anything links to the path form.

## All of it needs the nameserver move

A wildcard DNS record and a Worker route both live in the zone. Same
prerequisite as everything else in that section.

## Scope

**No implementation.** The publish step belongs on top of #89, which is still
open and rewrites `bin/build-sites.py` — stacking on it would be the one thing
the branch rules forbid. This PR touches only `docs/deploying.md`, which #89
does not, so the two are independent in either merge order.

The `member-sites.md` reference is deliberately plain text rather than a link,
because that file arrives with #89 and a link would be dead until it lands.

## Verified

- `bundle exec jekyll build` with the probe file — byte-identical passthrough,
  confirmed by `shasum`. Probe removed afterwards; the tree is clean.
- Every relative link in `docs/deploying.md` resolves.
- `python3 site/bin/test_no_secrets.py` — 12 tests, OK (2 skipped).
- Cloudflare claims are from their docs, cited in the section: Transform Rules
  regex availability, the asset-before-Worker ordering, `run_worker_first`, and
  route precedence.

